# Irreducible and indecomposable quiver representations
%%%
tag := "Chapter2/Discussion_quiver_irreducible_indecomposable"
number := false
%%%
As with representations of algebras, a nonzero representation $`(V_i)` of a quiver $`Q` is said to be irreducible if its only subrepresentations are $`(0)` and $`(V_i)` itself, and it is said to be indecomposable if it is not isomorphic to a direct sum of two nonzero representations.

